1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,500
Hello! The purpose of this video is to train new students how to use some of the basic equipment that you'll be using in the lab.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:14,500
For example, if you ever need to transfer liquid volumes, you'll probably use a pipetter, also known as a pipette.

3
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:23,940
Pipettes come in a range of sizes and can accurately transport volumes as small as 0.2 or as large as 1,000 microliters.

4
00:00:23,940 --> 00:00:31,940
The volume range of each pipette is shown on top of its plunger. For example, this P20 pipette can transfer 2 to 20 microliters.

5
00:00:31,940 --> 00:00:38,940
Sometimes, only the maximum volume of the pipette is shown on the plunger, but the volume range of all pipettes is 10-fold,

6
00:00:38,940 --> 00:00:42,940
so this pipette transfers 10 to 100 microliters.

7
00:00:42,940 --> 00:00:48,940
To change the volume setting on a pipette, rotate the black knob near the top, as shown here.

8
00:00:48,940 --> 00:00:54,940
Note that since this is a P1000 pipette, one of the digits is shown in red to indicate milliliters.

9
00:00:54,940 --> 00:01:06,510
The other two digits are shown in black for microliters. Right now, the pipette is set to 1 ml.

10
00:01:06,510 --> 00:01:13,510
On a P20 pipette, one of the digits is red to represent nanoliters, while the other two digits are shown in black for microliters.

11
00:01:13,510 --> 00:01:23,120
In this example, the pipette is set to 18.7 microliters.

12
00:01:23,120 --> 00:01:28,120
On a P2 pipette, two of the digits are shown in red, and one is in black for microliters.

13
00:01:28,120 --> 00:01:33,120
In this example, the pipette is currently set to 1.32 microliters.

14
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:40,780
Just as pipettes have unique volume ranges, they also require unique tips.

15
00:01:40,780 --> 00:01:44,780
For example, this P1000 requires large, blue tips.

16
00:01:44,780 --> 00:01:47,780
Always make sure that the tips fit on snugly.

17
00:01:47,780 --> 00:01:56,630
If the tips fit on loosely or fall off, then they won't work properly, and you'll need to find some other tips.

18
00:01:56,630 --> 00:01:59,630
Pipettes use a two-stop plunger system to transfer liquids.

19
00:01:59,630 --> 00:02:03,630
The first stop, as shown here, is used to draw up the liquid.

20
00:02:03,630 --> 00:02:08,630
The second stop is used to completely eject the liquid from the tip.

21
00:02:08,630 --> 00:02:14,630
So, once again, the first stop is used to draw up liquid, while the second stop is used to eject it.

22
00:02:14,630 --> 00:02:20,630
When transferring a sample, begin by pushing the plunger down to the first stop to eject all of the air.

23
00:02:20,630 --> 00:02:23,630
Then submerge the tip and release the plunger.

24
00:02:23,630 --> 00:02:29,630
As you eject the sample, push all the way to the second stop to make sure that all the liquid leaves the tip.

25
00:02:29,630 --> 00:02:36,630
When you are done using the tip, eject it into a sharps box using the ejector button, as shown here.

26
00:02:36,630 --> 00:02:42,630
If your pipette does not have a working ejector button, you can always take off the tip manually by hand.

27
00:02:42,630 --> 00:02:48,630
If you ever think a pipette isn't accurate, you can test it using an analytical balance.

28
00:02:48,630 --> 00:02:54,630
In this example, we're going to test a P1000 pipette by setting it to 700 microliters.

29
00:02:54,630 --> 00:03:02,630
Since the density of water is 1 gram per ml, 700 microliters of water should weigh 700 milligrams.

30
00:03:02,630 --> 00:03:08,630
Begin by tearing a weighboat and then slowly transferring the liquid volume to the weighboat.

31
00:03:08,630 --> 00:03:16,630
Make sure you completely eject all of the liquid, then close the doors on the analytical balance and wait for the reading to stabilize.

32
00:03:16,630 --> 00:03:23,630
In this case, the reading is only 541 milligrams, which is much less than the expected 700 milligrams.

33
00:03:23,630 --> 00:03:27,630
Therefore, the pipette is in dire need of calibration.

34
00:03:27,630 --> 00:03:34,630
You should no longer use the pipette for any experiments and immediately let your supervisor know that the pipette needs to be calibrated.

35
00:03:34,630 --> 00:03:43,420
If you need to transfer volumes larger than 1 ml, you'll need to use a pipette aid.

36
00:03:43,420 --> 00:03:48,420
Pipette aids use much larger tips with volume ranges from 2 to 50 ml.

37
00:03:48,420 --> 00:03:54,420
Begin by tightly inserting the tip into the pipette aid, then submerge the tip into the liquid.

38
00:03:54,420 --> 00:03:58,420
Draw liquid up with the top button and eject liquid with the bottom button.

39
00:03:58,420 --> 00:04:03,420
If at any point you draw liquid too far up, the pipette will stop working.

40
00:04:03,420 --> 00:04:09,420
This is because all pipette aids use an air filter to protect themselves from liquid contamination.

41
00:04:09,420 --> 00:04:17,420
Therefore, to get the pipette aid working again, you have to disassemble the pipette head and remove the clogged filter, as shown here.

42
00:04:17,420 --> 00:04:23,050
Once you've removed the clogged filter, you'll need to replace it with a new filter.

43
00:04:23,050 --> 00:04:28,050
Note that syringe filters designed for liquid will not work properly in a pipette aid.

44
00:04:28,050 --> 00:04:33,050
You must use the air filter specifically designed for your pipette aid.

45
00:04:33,050 --> 00:04:38,050
Once you have removed the old clogged filter and thoroughly dried all the plastic pieces,

46
00:04:38,050 --> 00:04:44,050
reassemble the pipette aid, making sure that the new filter and all the rubber pieces are in the correct orientation.

47
00:04:45,050 --> 00:04:49,050
Once you put it back together, test it with some liquid to make sure that it works.

48
00:04:49,050 --> 00:04:58,300
If the pipette aid continues to malfunction, let your supervisor know immediately.

49
00:04:58,300 --> 00:05:04,300
In addition to transferring liquid volumes, you also have to weigh out a variety of chemicals in your research.

50
00:05:04,300 --> 00:05:06,300
To do so, we use balances.

51
00:05:06,300 --> 00:05:14,300
Most labs have two types of balances, a crude balance for large masses and an analytical balance for very small masses.

52
00:05:14,300 --> 00:05:21,300
It is very important that you only weigh large masses on the crude balance and small masses on the analytical balance.

53
00:05:21,300 --> 00:05:25,300
This is because large masses will actually break the analytical balance,

54
00:05:25,300 --> 00:05:30,300
while the crude balance is not accurate enough to precisely weigh small masses.

55
00:05:30,300 --> 00:05:35,740
To weigh masses larger than one gram, use a weighboat.

56
00:05:35,740 --> 00:05:42,740
Insert the weighboat into the analytical balance, close all the doors, and tear it to set the mass to zero.

57
00:05:42,740 --> 00:05:44,740
Then add your chemical.

58
00:05:44,740 --> 00:05:48,740
To weigh masses smaller than a gram, use a weighing paper.

59
00:05:48,740 --> 00:05:54,740
Weighing papers are preferred because they cling less to small powders and crystals.

60
00:05:54,740 --> 00:06:01,740
Transfer your chemical to the weigh paper using a clean spatula or scupula, as shown here.

61
00:06:01,740 --> 00:06:06,740
Slowly add the chemical to the weigh paper to prevent going over the desired weight.

62
00:06:06,740 --> 00:06:19,120
Check the exact weight by closing the doors on the analytical balance and giving it a few seconds to stabilize.

63
00:06:19,120 --> 00:06:25,120
As long as your spatula is clean, you may return any excess chemical to the original bottle it came from.

64
00:06:25,120 --> 00:06:32,120
If you are ever weighing out a chemical and you accidentally spill some of it on the balance or bench, you'll need to clean it up immediately.

65
00:06:32,120 --> 00:06:36,120
Begin by removing your weighed-out chemical from the balance.

66
00:06:36,120 --> 00:06:40,120
Then use a brush to clean up any spilled powder or crystals.

67
00:06:40,120 --> 00:06:46,620
Once you have it all cleaned up, make sure you properly dispose of the chemical.

68
00:06:47,620 --> 00:06:52,620
Just like a pipetter, an analytical balance can also become inaccurate over time.

69
00:06:52,620 --> 00:06:59,620
To test the accuracy of an analytical balance, you'll need two standardized weights at the upper and lower bounds of the balance.

70
00:06:59,620 --> 00:07:03,620
In this case, we're using 2 and 50 gram weights.

71
00:07:03,620 --> 00:07:11,620
Notice that the 2 gram weight is reading 1.998 grams, which is less than 1% inaccurate, so that's pretty good.

72
00:07:12,620 --> 00:07:17,620
Next we test the 50 gram weight, which weighs out to be 49.95 grams.

73
00:07:17,620 --> 00:07:23,620
This is also greater than 99.9% accurate, so the balance is working properly.

74
00:07:23,620 --> 00:07:27,620
If these readings were significantly off, we would need to calibrate the balance.

75
00:07:27,620 --> 00:07:33,620
Refer the manufacturer's instructions on how to do so, or let your supervisor know immediately.

76
00:07:33,620 --> 00:07:40,620
So just to review, use crude balances for large weights, which is typically anything over about 5 grams.

77
00:07:41,620 --> 00:07:47,620
Use analytical balances for any small weights, anything from 0.1 milligram all the way up to a gram.

78
00:07:47,620 --> 00:07:55,480
And please clean off the balances and the bench if you spill any chemicals.

79
00:07:55,480 --> 00:07:58,480
Centrifuges are also commonly found in most labs.

80
00:07:58,480 --> 00:08:04,480
They're used to separate particles of different densities by spinning them rapidly to generate a high g-force.

81
00:08:04,480 --> 00:08:10,480
Since most centrifuges do operate at rather high speeds, it is important that you know how to properly use them

82
00:08:10,480 --> 00:08:14,480
to avoid damaging the centrifuge or harming yourself.

83
00:08:14,480 --> 00:08:21,480
The most important thing you need to know about using a centrifuge is that any samples you put on the rotor must be properly balanced.

84
00:08:21,480 --> 00:08:27,480
Use an analytical balance to make sure the weights of your samples are equivalent and adjust them as necessary.

85
00:08:27,480 --> 00:08:32,480
If you ever have an odd number of samples, you can always use a tube of water to even things out.

86
00:08:32,480 --> 00:08:38,480
When putting your samples on the rotor, make sure they're exactly opposite one another, otherwise you'll get an imbalance error.

87
00:08:38,480 --> 00:08:42,480
Next, put a lid on the rotor just in case any of your samples leak out.

88
00:08:42,480 --> 00:08:49,480
To set the speed on the centrifuge, always begin by selecting either g-force or RPM for revolutions per minute.

89
00:08:49,480 --> 00:08:54,480
Note that g-force may also be shown as RCF for relative centrifugal force.

90
00:08:54,480 --> 00:08:58,480
Next, set the time on the centrifuge and start the run.

91
00:08:58,480 --> 00:09:05,480
You should always stick around to watch the centrifuge reach maximum speed just in case there's any kind of imbalance error.

92
00:09:05,480 --> 00:09:11,480
Once the centrifuge has finished, wait for the rotor to completely stop spinning, then remove your samples.

93
00:09:11,480 --> 00:09:16,480
You should always check your samples to make sure the desired sedimentation has occurred.

94
00:09:16,480 --> 00:09:21,480
When you're done with the centrifuge, put the rotor lid back in place and close the main lid.

95
00:09:21,480 --> 00:09:28,210
Whenever you're using larger centrifuges that use buckets or 50 ml tubes,

96
00:09:28,210 --> 00:09:33,210
it's important that you only fill them up to about 80% of their maximum value.

97
00:09:34,210 --> 00:09:39,210
For instance, here we're only filling up the 50 ml tube to 40 ml.

98
00:09:39,210 --> 00:09:43,210
This is because tubes and buckets do not have perfect seals.

99
00:09:43,210 --> 00:09:48,210
Therefore, when they're spun at high speeds, they can leak out if they're overfilled.

100
00:09:48,210 --> 00:09:52,210
So to prevent making a huge mess that you have to clean up in the centrifuge,

101
00:09:52,210 --> 00:09:59,210
only fill up 50 ml tubes to 40 ml and fill 600 ml buckets to around 400 ml.

102
00:09:59,210 --> 00:10:06,210
So just to review, when using a centrifuge, always make sure that your samples are properly balanced.

103
00:10:06,210 --> 00:10:10,210
And finally, when using 50 ml tubes or large buckets,

104
00:10:10,210 --> 00:10:16,210
only fill them to 80% of their maximum volume to prevent any spills that might happen in the centrifuge.

105
00:10:16,210 --> 00:10:24,700
If a spill does occur, make sure that you clean it up immediately.

106
00:10:24,700 --> 00:10:27,700
The most important thing you need to know about a pH meter

107
00:10:27,700 --> 00:10:31,700
is that the electrode must be stored in electrode storage solution in between uses.

108
00:10:31,700 --> 00:10:36,700
If the electrode storage solution runs dry, then the pH meter will stop working properly

109
00:10:36,700 --> 00:10:39,700
and the electrode may even break.

110
00:10:39,700 --> 00:10:42,700
If you ever notice that the storage solution has run dry,

111
00:10:42,700 --> 00:10:46,700
immediately fill it back up with fresh electrode storage solution.

112
00:10:46,700 --> 00:10:49,700
Several companies offer pre-made storage solutions,

113
00:10:49,700 --> 00:10:57,700
or you can make your own by mixing 100 mg of potassium chloride with 10 ml of the pH-4 standard solution.

114
00:10:57,700 --> 00:11:06,530
When you are ready to use the electrode, remove it from the storage solution

115
00:11:06,530 --> 00:11:09,530
and thoroughly dry it with a chem wipe.

116
00:11:09,530 --> 00:11:14,530
Next, rinse off the electrode with milli-q water and dry it again.

117
00:11:14,530 --> 00:11:21,610
To calibrate the meter, pour a little bit of the standard solution into a 15 ml tube.

118
00:11:21,610 --> 00:11:25,610
This prevents the main standard solution from becoming contaminated.

119
00:11:25,610 --> 00:11:34,100
You can refer to the manufacturer's instructions for specific protocols on how to calibrate your pH meter.

120
00:11:34,100 --> 00:11:42,100
However, in general, pH standard solutions of 4, 7, and 10 are usually used to calibrate pH meters.

121
00:11:42,100 --> 00:11:46,100
When calibrating the meter, it is important that you use the two standard solutions

122
00:11:46,100 --> 00:11:49,100
that are closest to the pH of your buffer.

123
00:11:49,100 --> 00:11:53,100
For example, if you plan on making a sample with a pH of 6,

124
00:11:53,100 --> 00:11:56,100
you want to calibrate with the 4 and 7 standards.

125
00:11:56,100 --> 00:12:00,100
However, if the pH of your buffer will be 8.5,

126
00:12:00,100 --> 00:12:05,100
then you will want to calibrate the meter with the pH 7 and 10 standard solutions.

127
00:12:05,100 --> 00:12:10,100
To measure pH, completely submerge the tip of the electrode into your solution

128
00:12:10,100 --> 00:12:13,100
and ensure that it is vigorously mixed.

129
00:12:13,100 --> 00:12:16,100
The best way to do this is to use a stir bar and a stir plate,

130
00:12:16,100 --> 00:12:19,100
but you can do it with your hand if those are unavailable.

131
00:12:19,100 --> 00:12:24,100
Remember to press the read button on the pH meter and give it a little while to stabilize.

132
00:12:24,100 --> 00:12:28,100
Once the reading has stabilized, you can adjust the pH of the solution

133
00:12:28,100 --> 00:12:31,100
with hydrochloric acid or sodium hydroxide.

134
00:12:31,100 --> 00:12:37,100
Note that hydrochloric acid will decrease the pH while sodium hydroxide will raise the pH.

135
00:12:37,100 --> 00:12:43,100
In either case, add the acid or base in small amounts and vigorously mix the solution after you add them.

136
00:12:43,100 --> 00:12:47,100
It is important that you add the acid and base in very small amounts

137
00:12:47,100 --> 00:12:52,100
such that you don't overshoot and have to add excess acid and base.

138
00:12:52,100 --> 00:12:55,100
This will prevent you from adding too much salt to your solution.

139
00:12:55,100 --> 00:13:05,650
Once you are done titrating your solution, remember to put the electrode back into the storage solution.

140
00:13:05,650 --> 00:13:17,100
I'd like to stop for a moment to talk specifically about buffers.

141
00:13:17,100 --> 00:13:21,100
The purpose of a buffer is to maintain the pH of a solution,

142
00:13:21,100 --> 00:13:26,100
even though a reaction may be occurring that would otherwise raise or lower the pH.

143
00:13:26,100 --> 00:13:29,100
The most important thing you need to know about buffers

144
00:13:29,100 --> 00:13:33,100
is that they are only effective within specific pH ranges.

145
00:13:33,100 --> 00:13:40,100
For example, biz Tris buffer is only effective between pH 5.8 and 7.3.

146
00:13:40,100 --> 00:13:46,100
Therefore, if you titrate it to pH 8, it will not resist pH change effectively.

147
00:13:46,100 --> 00:13:53,580
Instead, if you need to make a buffer at pH 8, it would be better to use Tris HC out,

148
00:13:53,580 --> 00:13:56,580
which has a range from 7.2 to 9.

149
00:13:56,580 --> 00:13:59,580
It is important to mention that the buffers shown in this table

150
00:13:59,580 --> 00:14:03,580
are only a small sampling of all the buffers available.

151
00:14:03,580 --> 00:14:07,580
You can find many more online, which may be useful for your experiments.

152
00:14:07,580 --> 00:14:13,820
Aside from the pH range of a buffer, you should also consider its chemical reactivity.

153
00:14:13,820 --> 00:14:20,820
For example, Tris buffers are weak chelators. That means they will weakly bind metal ions.

154
00:14:20,820 --> 00:14:24,820
In addition, Heaps buffers generate free radicals

155
00:14:24,820 --> 00:14:28,820
that can significantly interfere with your chemical reaction if exposed to sunlight.

156
00:14:28,820 --> 00:14:34,070
Therefore, you should always remember to check the pH range

157
00:14:34,070 --> 00:14:38,070
and the reactivity of your buffer just to make sure it is ideal

158
00:14:38,070 --> 00:14:40,070
for the reaction that you are trying to do.

159
00:14:40,070 --> 00:14:44,450
If you work with any hazardous chemicals in your research,

160
00:14:44,450 --> 00:14:47,450
you probably need to use the fume hood at some point.

161
00:14:47,450 --> 00:14:52,450
To begin working in the fume hood, raise the sash to the maximum safe height.

162
00:14:52,450 --> 00:14:56,450
Note that if you raise it too high, the fume hood will not work properly

163
00:14:56,450 --> 00:14:59,450
and some vapors may escape into the lab.

164
00:14:59,450 --> 00:15:02,450
When you raise the sash, it should start drawing air inwards.

165
00:15:02,450 --> 00:15:05,450
If there is no air flow or any alarms go off in the hood,

166
00:15:05,450 --> 00:15:09,450
you should let your supervisor know immediately and not use the fume hood.

167
00:15:09,450 --> 00:15:11,450
When performing experiments in the fume hood,

168
00:15:11,450 --> 00:15:14,450
you should always work at least 6 inches deep.

169
00:15:14,450 --> 00:15:18,450
This will prevent any vapors from accidentally escaping from the fume hood.

170
00:15:18,450 --> 00:15:23,450
In addition, you should always keep the fume hood as clean and empty as possible.

171
00:15:23,450 --> 00:15:27,450
This will prevent hazardous chemicals from cross-reacting from one another

172
00:15:27,450 --> 00:15:31,450
and it will ensure that the vents that you can see in the back of the fume hood here

173
00:15:31,450 --> 00:15:35,450
will remain unblocked and working properly.

174
00:15:35,450 --> 00:15:39,450
Remember, if these vents become blocked, the fume hood may lose suction

175
00:15:39,450 --> 00:15:43,450
and hazardous vapors may escape into the lab.

176
00:15:43,450 --> 00:15:46,450
So, to review, before working in the fume hood,

177
00:15:46,450 --> 00:15:49,450
always check the air flow of the hood.

178
00:15:49,450 --> 00:15:52,450
You should also work 6 inches deep at all times

179
00:15:52,450 --> 00:15:55,450
and try not to block any of the vents inside of the fume hood.

180
00:15:55,450 --> 00:16:01,010
Another commonly used piece of equipment in all of our labs

181
00:16:01,010 --> 00:16:03,010
is the Ultra Pure Water System.

182
00:16:03,010 --> 00:16:06,010
Ultra Pure Water should be used for all of your buffers

183
00:16:06,010 --> 00:16:10,010
since tap water and even distilled water may have some contaminants

184
00:16:10,010 --> 00:16:12,010
that could interfere with your reactions.

185
00:16:12,010 --> 00:16:16,010
To collect Ultra Pure Water, place the dispensing hose inside your bottle

186
00:16:16,010 --> 00:16:19,010
and pull the trigger as shown here.

187
00:16:19,010 --> 00:16:22,010
Do not let bottles fill up on the bench.

188
00:16:22,010 --> 00:16:27,010
Instead, put them in the sink just in case you forget about them and they overflow.

189
00:16:27,010 --> 00:16:31,010
Please note that if you do leave a bottle on the bench and it overflows,

190
00:16:31,010 --> 00:16:37,010
it can flood the lab, thereby damaging or ruining many expensive pieces of lab equipment.

191
00:16:37,010 --> 00:16:41,010
When collecting Ultra Pure Water, you should always check the readout on the system

192
00:16:41,010 --> 00:16:44,010
to ensure that the water is ultra pure.

193
00:16:44,010 --> 00:16:48,010
Ultra Pure Water has a resistivity of 18.2 mega ohms per centimeter,

194
00:16:48,010 --> 00:16:51,010
as shown on the readout here.

195
00:16:51,010 --> 00:16:55,010
If the resistivity is any lower, that means your water is not ultra pure

196
00:16:55,010 --> 00:16:59,010
and one of the filters in the system will likely need to be replaced.

197
00:16:59,010 --> 00:17:03,010
Notify your supervisor if this is the case.

198
00:17:03,010 --> 00:17:07,010
Also notify your supervisor if you observe any alarms on the system.

199
00:17:07,010 --> 00:17:12,010
Once you have your Ultra Pure Water, you probably use a stir plate to make your solution.

200
00:17:12,010 --> 00:17:17,010
Begin by inserting a stir bar that isn't too big or too small for the bottle.

201
00:17:17,010 --> 00:17:22,010
Then slowly turn up the RPMs and add your chemical that you wish to dissolve.

202
00:17:27,040 --> 00:17:30,040
In some cases, you may wish to heat the sample as well.

203
00:17:30,040 --> 00:17:35,040
Please remember to use extreme caution when heating liquids on a hot plate.

204
00:17:35,040 --> 00:17:39,040
For example, do not heat water above its boiling point,

205
00:17:39,040 --> 00:17:44,040
which can be 90 to 100 degrees C unless you are explicitly trying to boil it.

206
00:17:44,040 --> 00:17:48,040
Volatile solvents should only be heated in the chemical fume hood

207
00:17:48,040 --> 00:17:52,040
to avoid inhalation of any harmful vapors.

208
00:17:52,040 --> 00:17:58,040
Finally, hot plates should not be left on at high temperatures for extended periods of time.

209
00:17:58,040 --> 00:18:02,040
For example, try not to leave a hot plate on overnight,

210
00:18:02,040 --> 00:18:08,040
since it is not uncommon for hot plates to overheat and create a fire hazard when left unattended.

